// assets/data/replay.ron — replay-log output, for the *driver*.
//
// This is the only content file the simulation never reads. It configures where
// the shipped binary puts the command log of a finished match; it says nothing
// about how a match plays, and it is deliberately kept out of `Content` (and so
// out of the content fingerprint) for two reasons:
//
//   * a fingerprint is a claim about the match, and a directory name is not part
//     of one — hashing it would mean that turning logging on, or moving the
//     output directory, invalidated every replay already on disk;
//   * a path in sim state is one step away from a filename influencing a hash,
//     and the sim must stay render-free, clock-free and file-free.
//
// The file is optional: if it is missing, replay logging is simply off (which is
// also the default below). A file that is *present but malformed* is an error —
// a missing optional config is a state; a broken one is a mistake.
(
    // **Off by default.** A replay per run is unbounded growth on disk, and the
    // machine this is developed on has ~1 GiB free. Turn it on deliberately.
    enabled: false,

    // Where logs go. A relative path is relative to the process's working
    // directory (the repo root when run with `cargo run`).
    dir: "replays",

    // Filename: `<prefix>-<unix seconds>-seed<seed>.ron`, plus `-1`, `-2`, ...
    // if that name is already taken. The timestamp and the seed do **not** make
    // a name unique — two matches can finish in the same second with the same
    // seed — so the writer never trusts the name: it claims the file with
    // `create_new`, which fails if the file exists, and walks the suffix until
    // it claims an unused one. It never overwrites a log.
    prefix: "onus",

    // How many taken names to walk before giving up. Giving up is reported and
    // loses the *new* log; overwriting would lose an old one, which is worse.
    max_collisions: 64,
)
